import Base from "../../layouts/Base.astro";
---
<Base
  title="Why I started LightPhotos — LightPhotos Blog"
  desc="A Lightroom license stopped working, and Apple Photos did not open years of my photos. One afternoon, I gave up and built the small editor that I actually needed."
  active="blogs"
>
  <main class="lp-page">
    <article class="lp-article">
      <header class="lp-article-head">
        <p class="lp-article-meta"><a href="/blogs.html">Blogs</a> &middot; <time datetime="2026-06-23">June 23, 2026</time></p>
        <h1>Why I started LightPhotos</h1>
        <p class="lp-article-dek">
          A Lightroom license stopped working, and I could not open years of
          photos from Apple Photos. One afternoon, I gave up and built the
          small editor that I actually needed.
        </p>
        <p class="lp-article-byline">Andrew Yao &middot; 4 min read</p>
      </header>

      <aside class="lp-tldr">
        <p class="lp-tldr-label">TL;DR</p>
        <p>
          I paid for Lightroom 6. After a macOS upgrade, I could not install it
          again. I moved to Apple Photos. A few years and a few upgrades
          later, I lost years of photos, because the newer version did not open
          the library that held them. The free editors that I tried, for
          example Darktable, wanted me to learn a whole craft. I only wanted to
          send some pictures to my friends. So I wrote down the few things that I
          actually needed. Then I built the smallest editor that does
          them. Coding agents wrote most of the code.
        </p>
      </aside>

      <h2>What I learned</h2>
      <ul class="lp-article-list">
        <li>
          <strong>Keep your photos as plain files.</strong> A backup helps only
          if an app can open it. Almost all apps can open ordinary files on a
          drive, now or ten years from now.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Buying software once does not mean that you can always use it.</strong>
          A macOS upgrade stopped my Lightroom license. The result was the same
          as a cancelled subscription, and nothing warned me before it occurred.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>More features made the free editors harder for me.</strong>
          They could do everything. That is why I could not get started with
          them.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Write the list before the code.</strong> I wrote down what
          the first version had to do. That list helped me say no to everything
          else.
        </li>
      </ul>

      <h2>Nothing quite fit</h2>
      <p>
        I am an amateur photographer. I like taking pictures with my Sony
        full-frame camera. I shoot RAW. This means that the camera saves the
        unprocessed sensor data. So most of my shots need a little work
        before I can share them.
      </p>

      <figure class="lp-figure">
        <svg viewBox="0 0 640 224" role="img" aria-label="Five options and the reason that each one failed. Lightroom Classic: the installer stopped after a macOS upgrade. Luminar AI: it does not use the keyboard-first method that I use to edit. Apple Photos: it is beautiful and fast, but it has one problem that I cannot accept. Darktable and similar editors: the features that I need are behind many features that I do not need. A Lightroom subscription: ten dollars each month for an app that I open a few times each year. All five options cause me to build a small editor myself.">
          <text class="lp-fig-label" x="0" y="12">WHAT I TRIED FIRST</text>

          <rect class="lp-fig-box" x="0" y="24" width="190" height="24" rx="5"></rect>
          <text class="lp-fig-s" x="12" y="40">Lightroom 6</text>
          <circle class="lp-fig-bad" cx="204" cy="36" r="3"></circle>
          <text class="lp-fig-s" x="216" y="40">I own it. After a clean OS upgrade, the Lightroom 6 installer does not run.</text>

          <rect class="lp-fig-box" x="0" y="54" width="190" height="24" rx="5"></rect>
          <text class="lp-fig-s" x="12" y="70">Luminar AI</text>
          <circle class="lp-fig-bad" cx="204" cy="66" r="3"></circle>
          <text class="lp-fig-s" x="216" y="70">Good, but the experience and performance are not what I wanted.</text>

          <rect class="lp-fig-box" x="0" y="84" width="190" height="24" rx="5"></rect>
          <text class="lp-fig-s" x="12" y="100">Apple Photos</text>
          <circle class="lp-fig-bad" cx="204" cy="96" r="3"></circle>
          <text class="lp-fig-s" x="216" y="100">Beautiful and fast, but it has one problem that I cannot accept.</text>

          <rect class="lp-fig-box" x="0" y="114" width="190" height="24" rx="5"></rect>
          <text class="lp-fig-s" x="12" y="130">Darktable and friends</text>
          <circle class="lp-fig-bad" cx="204" cy="126" r="3"></circle>
          <text class="lp-fig-s" x="216" y="130">All that I need, behind all that I do not need.</text>

          <rect class="lp-fig-box" x="0" y="144" width="190" height="24" rx="5"></rect>
          <text class="lp-fig-s" x="12" y="160">Lightroom subscription</text>
          <circle class="lp-fig-bad" cx="204" cy="156" r="3"></circle>
          <text class="lp-fig-s" x="216" y="160">$10 each month for an app that I open only sometimes.</text>

          <line class="lp-fig-line" x1="95" y1="168" x2="95" y2="184"></line>
          <path class="lp-fig-arrow" d="M90 184 L100 184 L95 194 Z"></path>
          <rect class="lp-fig-box-accent" x="0" y="196" width="190" height="28" rx="5"></rect>
          <text class="lp-fig-t" x="95" y="215" text-anchor="middle">Build a small one myself</text>
        </svg>
      </figure>

      <p>
        I really do like Apple Photos. It looks great, it is fast, and it does
        nearly everything that I wanted. If you use it and you are satisfied, I
        completely understand why.
      </p>
      <p>
        But Apple Photos has one large problem for me. Your full library is in
        a single file, in a format that only Apple Photos can read. I learned
        what that means from a bad experience. A newer version of macOS did
        not open the Photos libraries on my older backups. Several years of
        pictures went with them. If you lost photos after an upgrade, you know how bad
        that feels.
      </p>
      <p>
        I cannot get past that problem. The features and the speed are fine. But
        all of them depend on a format that I do not control. I am not
        willing to take that risk again.
      </p>

      <figure class="lp-figure">
        <svg viewBox="0 0 640 208" role="img" aria-label="Two paths for the same photos. In a locked library, your photos become one library file, and they are lost after an upgrade. As ordinary files, your photos stay ordinary files, and any app can open them.">
          <text class="lp-fig-label" x="0" y="12">A BACKUP THAT YOU CANNOT OPEN IS NOT A BACKUP</text>

          <rect class="lp-fig-panel" x="0" y="24" width="640" height="84" rx="8"></rect>
          <text class="lp-fig-t" x="16" y="46">Locked in a library</text>
          <rect class="lp-fig-box" x="16" y="58" width="176" height="30" rx="5"></rect>
          <text class="lp-fig-s" x="104" y="78" text-anchor="middle">Your photos</text>
          <line class="lp-fig-line" x1="192" y1="73" x2="214" y2="73"></line>
          <path class="lp-fig-arrow" d="M214 68 L224 73 L214 78 Z"></path>
          <rect class="lp-fig-box" x="224" y="58" width="176" height="30" rx="5"></rect>
          <text class="lp-fig-s" x="312" y="78" text-anchor="middle">One library file</text>
          <line class="lp-fig-line" x1="400" y1="73" x2="422" y2="73"></line>
          <path class="lp-fig-arrow" d="M422 68 L432 73 L422 78 Z"></path>
          <rect class="lp-fig-bad-stroke" x="432" y="58" width="176" height="30" rx="5"></rect>
          <text class="lp-fig-s" x="520" y="78" text-anchor="middle">Lost after an upgrade</text>

          <rect class="lp-fig-panel" x="0" y="116" width="640" height="84" rx="8"></rect>
          <text class="lp-fig-t" x="16" y="138">Kept as ordinary files</text>
          <rect class="lp-fig-box" x="16" y="150" width="176" height="30" rx="5"></rect>
          <text class="lp-fig-s" x="104" y="170" text-anchor="middle">Your photos</text>
          <line class="lp-fig-line" x1="192" y1="165" x2="214" y2="165"></line>
          <path class="lp-fig-arrow" d="M214 160 L224 165 L214 170 Z"></path>
          <rect class="lp-fig-box" x="224" y="150" width="176" height="30" rx="5"></rect>
          <text class="lp-fig-s" x="312" y="170" text-anchor="middle">Ordinary files</text>
          <line class="lp-fig-line" x1="400" y1="165" x2="422" y2="165"></line>
          <path class="lp-fig-arrow" d="M422 160 L432 165 L422 170 Z"></path>
          <rect class="lp-fig-box-accent" x="432" y="150" width="176" height="30" rx="5"></rect>
          <text class="lp-fig-s" x="520" y="170" text-anchor="middle">Open them in any app</text>
        </svg>
        <figcaption>
          In the two cases, the photos and the backup drive are the same. The
          difference is whether you need the app of one company to open them.
        </figcaption>
      </figure>

      <p>
        A single library also does not fit how I keep my files. My main
        drive holds recent work. Older shoots move to other drives.
        I do not want hundreds of gigabytes on the main drive of my laptop. I
        also do not want them in a library that I possibly cannot open in a
        few years.
      </p>

      <h2>So I built one</h2>
      <p>
        That afternoon, I started to write a basic version myself. I did not
        try to copy Lightroom. I wanted the smallest thing that could get these
        photos to my friends. So I wrote the list before I wrote code.
      </p>
      <ol class="lp-article-list">
        <li>
          <strong>Open a folder and show the photos in it.</strong> My photos stay as ordinary files on the drive that holds them. So I can always open them with something else.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Rate photos from 1 to 5,</strong> so that I can go
          through a shoot quickly and mark the best ones.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Filter by rating,</strong> so that I see and edit only the photos that I like.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Adjust exposure, highlights, shadows, and the other basic settings.</strong>
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Keep all adjustments non-destructive.</strong> The editor
          never touches the original file, so I can always undo an edit or
          start again.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Export selected photos to JPEG,</strong> ready to send.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Run on a Mac.</strong>
        </li>
      </ol>
      <p>
        That was all that it had to do. Anything that was not on the list
        stayed out of the first version. The written list is what helped me
        keep saying no.
      </p>

      <h2>What else I am trying to learn</h2>
      <p>
        I try to write very little of the code myself, and coding agents write
        most of it. That sounds simple, but doing it well is hard. An
        agent will happily give you code that compiles and runs. But that code can give
        the wrong result sometimes, or it can crash in a case that nobody
        tested. A photo editor shows these problems quickly. If a slider
        lags, or a folder of RAW files uses all your memory, you notice
        right away.
      </p>
      <p>
        So LightPhotos is two projects for me at once. It is the photo editor that
        I wanted. It is also how I learn what it takes to build
        software with agents and still care about quality and speed. Most of these
        posts are about that topic.
      </p>

      <h2>What comes next</h2>
      <p>
        In the next post, I will go through the big design decisions in
        LightPhotos and what I gave up for each one. If this sounds familiar,
        I would really like to hear how you dealt with it.
      </p>




